Now I understand the issue. In `mrb_debug_get_position`, when `irep->debug_info` is NULL, it sets `*lp = -1; *fp = NULL;` and returns FALSE. But when `irep` is NULL or `pc >= irep->ilen`, it returns FALSE **without setting** `*lp` and `*fp`. This means `loc.lineno` and `loc.filename` remain uninitialized in `each_backtrace`.

Looking at `each_backtrace`:
```c
struct backtrace_location loc;  // uninitialized
...
else {
  irep = ci->proc->body.irep;
  if (!irep) continue;
  if (mrb->c->cibase[i].pc) {
    pc = &mrb->c->cibase[i].pc[-1];
  }
  else {
    continue;
  }
  idx = (uint32_t)(pc - irep->iseq);
  mrb_debug_get_position(mrb, irep, idx, &loc.lineno, &loc.filename);
}
```

If `idx >= irep->ilen`, `mrb_debug_get_position` returns FALSE without setting `loc.lineno` and `loc.filename`. Then:
```c
if (loc.lineno == -1) {  // reads uninitialized loc.lineno
  ...
}
if (!loc.filename) {  // reads uninitialized loc.filename
  loc.filename = "(unknown)";
}
```

This is the use-of-uninitialized-value. The crash state is `each_backtrace -> mrb_keep_backtrace -> mrb_exc_raise`.

So I need to craft Ruby code that:
1. Raises an exception (triggering `mrb_exc_raise` → `mrb_keep_backtrace` → `each_backtrace`)
2. Has a call frame where `pc >= irep->ilen` (so `mrb_debug_get_position` returns FALSE without initializing `loc`)

Let me look at how `pc` is set. In `each_backtrace`, `pc = &mrb->c->cibase[i].pc[-1]` and `idx = pc - irep->iseq`. If `pc` points to the end of the iseq (one past the last instruction), then `idx == irep->ilen`, which would trigger the bug.

Let me look at the mruby version and check how the fuzzer is built. Let me first check if there's a built binary or if I need to build it.
